@Tutorial(time: 25) {
    @Intro(title: "Jumping and squashing monsters") {
        In this part, we'll add the ability to jump and squash the monsters. In the
        next lesson, we'll make the player die when a monster hits them on the
        ground.

        First, we have to change a few settings related to physics interactions.
        Enter the world of
        [physics layers](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/physics/physics%5Fintroduction.html#doc-physics-introduction-collision-layers-and-masks).
    }
    @Section(title: "Controlling physics interactions") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Physics bodies have access to two complementary properties: layers
            and masks. Layers define on which physics layer(s) an object is.

            Masks control the layers that a body will listen to and detect. This
            affects collision detection. When you want two bodies to interact,
            you need at least one to have a mask corresponding to the other.

            The important point is that you can use layers and masks to filter
            physics interactions, control performance, and remove the need for
            extra conditions in your code.

            By default, all physics bodies and areas are set to both layer and
            mask `1`. This means they all collide with each other.

            Physics layers are represented by numbers, but we can give them
            names to keep track of what's what.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Let's give our physics layers a name. Go to _Project -> Project
                Settings_.

                In the left menu, navigate down to _Layer Names -> 3D Physics_.
                You can see a list of layers with a field next to each of them
                on the right. You can set their names there. Name the first
                three layers _player_, _enemies_, and _world_, respectively.

                Now, we can assign them to our physics nodes.

                > Tip: Naming the layers is purely for the editor's benefit
                > — the layers themselves are still just integers. But using
                > readable names makes the _Collision_ section of the
                > _Inspector_ far easier to scan.

                @Image(source: 03.physics_layers.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                In the _Main_ scene, select the `Ground` node. In the
                _Inspector_, expand the _Collision_ section. There, you can
                see the node's layers and masks as a grid of buttons.

                The ground is part of the world, so we want it to be part of
                the third layer. Tap the lit button to toggle **off** the
                first _Layer_ and toggle **on** the third one. Then, toggle
                **off** the _Mask_ by tapping on it.

                As mentioned before, the _Mask_ property allows a node to
                listen to interaction with other physics objects, but we
                don't need it to have collisions. `Ground` doesn't need to
                listen to anything; it's just there to prevent creatures
                from falling.

                Note that you can tap the "..." button on the right side of
                the properties to see a list of named checkboxes.

                @Image(source: 05.toggle_layer_and_mask.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Next up are the `Player` and the `Mob`. Open `player.tscn` by
                double-tapping the file in the _FileSystem_ dock.

                Select the _Player_ node and set its _Collision -> Mask_ to
                both "enemies" and "world". You can leave the default _Layer_
                property as it is, because the first layer is the "player"
                layer.

                @Image(source: 07.player_physics_mask.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Then, open the _Mob_ scene by double-tapping on `mob.tscn`
                and select the `Mob` node.

                Set its _Collision -> Layer_ to "enemies" and unset its
                _Collision -> Mask_, leaving the mask empty.

                These settings mean the monsters will move through one
                another. If you want the monsters to collide with and slide
                against each other, turn **on** the "enemies" mask.

                > Note: The mobs don't need to mask the "world" layer
                > because they only move on the XZ plane. We don't apply
                > any gravity to them by design.

                @Image(source: 08.mob_physics_mask.webp)
            }
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Jumping") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The jumping mechanic itself requires only two lines of code. Open
            the _Player_ script. We need a value to control the jump's
            strength and update `_physics_process()` to code the jump.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                After the line that defines `fall_acceleration`, at the top
                of the script, add the `jump_impulse` property.

                Then, inside `_physics_process()`, add a check that
                applies the jump impulse when the player is on the
                floor and just pressed the "jump" action.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-07.gd, previousFile: player-06.gd)
            }
            That's all you need to jump!

            The `is_on_floor()` method is a tool from the `CharacterBody3D`
            class. It returns `true` if the body collided with the floor in
            this frame. That's why we apply gravity to the _Player_: so we
            collide with the floor instead of floating over it like the
            monsters.

            If the character is on the floor and the player presses "jump",
            we instantly give them a lot of vertical speed. In games, you
            really want controls to be responsive and giving instant speed
            boosts like these, while unrealistic, feels great.

            > Tip: Notice that the Y axis is positive upwards in 3D. That's
            > unlike 2D, where the Y axis is positive downwards. The
            > `jump_impulse` value is a positive Y velocity, so the
            > character moves up — not down.
        }
    }
    @Section(title: "Squashing monsters") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Let's add the squash mechanic next. We're going to make the
            character bounce over monsters and kill them at the same time.

            We need to detect collisions with a monster and to differentiate
            them from collisions with the floor. To do so, we can use Godot's
            [group](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/groups.html#doc-groups)
            tagging feature.

            @Image(source: fix-me.png)
        }
        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Open the scene `mob.tscn` again and select the _Mob_ node.
                Go to the _Groups_ dock on the right to see a list of
                groups, which allows you to assign tags to nodes. Tap on
                the _+_ button to open the _Create new Group_ dialog.

                @Image(source: 09.groups_tab.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Enter "mob" in the _Name_ field and tap the _Ok_ button.

                The "mob" group is now shown under the _Scene Groups_
                section.

                @Image(source: 10.group_create_new.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                An icon appears in the _Scene_ dock to indicate the node is
                part of at least one group.

                We can now use the group from the code to distinguish
                collisions with monsters from collisions with the floor.

                > Note: A node can belong to multiple groups, and a single
                > group can hold many node types. Groups are commonly used
                > as light-weight tags for collision filtering, palette
                > swaps, achievement systems, and more.

                @Image(source: 11.group_scene_groups.webp)
            }
            @Step {
                Head back to the _Player_ script to code the squash and
                bounce.

                At the top of the script, we need another property,
                `bounce_impulse`. When squashing an enemy, we don't
                necessarily want the character to go as high up as when
                jumping.

                @Code(name: "player.gd", file: player-08.gd, previousFile: player-07.gd)
            }
            That's a lot of new functions. Here's some more information
            about them.

            The functions `get_slide_collision_count()` and
            `get_slide_collision()` both come from the
            [CharacterBody3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fcharacterbody3d.html#class-characterbody3d)
            class and are related to `move_and_slide()`.

            `get_slide_collision()` returns a
            [KinematicCollision3D](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fkinematiccollision3d.html#class-kinematiccollision3d)
            object that holds information about where and how the collision
            occurred. For example, we use its `get_collider` property to
            check if we collided with a "mob" by calling `is_in_group()` on
            it: `collision.get_collider().is_in_group("mob")`.

            > Note: The method `is_in_group()` is available on every
            > [Node](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class%5Fnode.html#class-node).

            To check that we are landing on the monster, we use the vector
            dot product: `Vector3.UP.dot(collision.get_normal()) > 0.1`.
            The collision normal is a 3D vector that is perpendicular to
            the plane where the collision occurred. The dot product allows
            us to compare it to the up direction.

            > Tip: With dot products, when the result is greater than `0`,
            > the two vectors are at an angle of fewer than 90 degrees. A
            > value higher than `0.1` tells us that we are roughly above
            > the monster — anything lower and we hit it from the side.

            After handling the squash and bounce logic, we terminate the
            loop early via the `break` statement to prevent further
            duplicate calls to `mob.squash()`, which may otherwise result
            in unintended bugs such as counting the score multiple times
            for one kill.

            @Step {
                We are calling one undefined function, `mob.squash()`, so
                we have to add it to the Mob class.

                Open the script `mob.gd` by double-tapping on it in the
                _FileSystem_ dock. At the top of the script, we want to
                define a new signal named `squashed`. And at the bottom,
                you can add the squash function, where we emit the signal
                and destroy the mob.

                @Code(name: "mob.gd", file: mob-05.gd, previousFile: mob-04.gd)
            }
            We will use the signal to add points to the score in the next
            lesson.

            With that, you should be able to kill monsters by jumping on
            them. You can press F5 to try the game and set `main.tscn` as
            your project's main scene.

            However, the player won't die yet. We'll work on that in the
            next part.
        }
    }
}
